The drops were all held at 60". The differences in detected drop height, though seemingly undetected by live and slo-motion visual analysis, seem to be attributable to potential damage to the drop tower as noted in https://github.com/vertical-cloud-lab/tensegrity-optimization/issues/92 This seems likely to be the issue as the second drop pin was broken during drop 6, where the data splits into two detected height changes (before and after).

The design defects in specimen two were not insignificant, and now that we've relatively smoothed out our printing process, our specimens are much more consistent. It seems like it would be a good idea to print two good specimens of the same structure and run tests on those rather than confirming that major defects effect specimen analysis, where the intention would be to determine if we can produce the same results relatively consistently between two relatively ideal prints.

